  {
    title: 'What a Thriving Property Actually Looks Like',
    duration: '4:12',
    thumb: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1500651230702-0e2d8a49d4e6?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80',
    desc: 'Most people can feel when a landscape is alive and when it isn\'t — they just can\'t say why. This walks through what biological activity looks like above and below ground, and why it changes everything you see.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Year One: What the First Season Actually Does',
    duration: '6:30',
    thumb: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1474366521946-c3d4b507abf2?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80',
    desc: 'Year one is mostly invisible. Root establishment, fungal networks, soil structure building — almost none of it shows up above ground until late in the season. Here\'s what we\'re actually watching for.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Why Fertilizing Is Already In Your Program',
    duration: '3:45',
    thumb: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1416879595882-3373a0480b5b?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80',
    desc: 'Most landscapers charge fertilizing as an add-on. We don\'t — and that\'s not generosity, it\'s how we think about soil. If we\'re not feeding the biology, we\'re not doing ecological work. We\'re just mowing.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Native Plants: Built for This Soil, This Rain, This Region',
    duration: '5:08',
    thumb: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1441974231531-c6227db76b6e?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80',
